  "step": [
    {
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1,
      "name": "Step 1",
      "text": "Discovering your basement floor isn't perfectly level can be a real headache, especially if you're planning new flooring. An uneven slab can lead to gaps, movement, and premature wear, particularly with rigid materials like tile or engineered wood. So, what's the game plan? First, you need a thorough assessment. A professional will check for moisture issues, as underlying dampness can affect how leveling compounds perform and can impact any new flooring. Next up is surface preparation. This means removing any loose paint or coatings, giving the floor a good clean, and addressing any existing cracks or spalls. Ignoring these can create weak points for your new floor. Depending on how uneven the floor is, the solution might involve patching compounds for smaller dips or a self-leveling underlayment for more significant slopes. Correctly applying these materials is crucial; it's a technique-sensitive job that impacts the long-term performance and look of your new floor. Skipping this step can void flooring warranties and lead to ongoing problems. Getting this right from the start is key to a durable and attractive basement floor. If your basement slab is giving you grief, HH Remodeling LLC can help evaluate your situation and ensure proper preparation before any new flooring goes down, serving areas like Denver and Aurora, Colorado."
    }
  ],
